  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p><b>Implement monitoring with CloudWatch Alarms to identify underperforming resources and automatically stop or remove those not needed</b> is correct because CloudWatch Alarms can be set up to detect underutilization. Automated actions, such as stopping or terminating instances based on alarm conditions, can efficiently reduce costs due to running idle resources.</p><p><b>Leverage AWS Trusted Advisor to highlight resources with low utilization</b> is also a highly effective solution. Trusted Advisor continuously assesses your AWS environment and recommends optimizations, including identifying underused EC2 instances, load balancers, and other services where cost savings are possible.</p><p><i>Develop a custom script that surveys running resources, calculates expenses, and reviews opportunities for enhancements</i> is not the most efficient approach. While it is possible, AWS offers built-in services like Trusted Advisor and CloudWatch which are more robust and require less manual intervention or maintenance.</p><p><i>Set up CloudWatch Logs to track resources with low activity, then power off or decommission those that are unnecessary</i> is incorrect because CloudWatch Logs is primarily for log data collection and analysis, not resource monitoring for optimization. CloudWatch Alarms is the proper tool for this function, as it is specifically designed to monitor metrics and alert or automate based upon them.</p><ul><li>Tip: Use AWS managed services like Trusted Advisor and CloudWatch to simplify cost optimization and monitoring, as they integrate with other AWS services and automate much of the process.</li><li>Remember: Logs are for capturing and searching logs, not for usage monitoring. Alarms are purpose-built for alerting and responding to resource utilization metrics.</li></ul>",
